# ctp-bridge configuration — copy to .ctp-bridge.conf on the box and edit.
#
# This file is the BOUNDARY. The wrapper (scripts/ctp-bridge.sh) and the
# PreToolUse hook (.claude/hooks/pretooluse-ctp.sh) both read it, and neither
# trusts caller-supplied environment for these values — an agent that can set an
# env var in the same call it makes could otherwise rewrite its own limits.
#
# .ctp-bridge.conf is gitignored: it lives on the box and is never committed. It
# holds NO secrets — only a box name, a verb list, and paths to deny. Real
# secret VALUES never appear here.
#
# Format: KEY=value, one per line. Lines starting with # are ignored. No shell
# expansion is performed on values.

# The single box this harness may deploy to, named EXACTLY (including the team
# suffix). Any mutating verb naming anything else is refused. One name, no
# wildcard, no list. Example shape: some-box_t02
CTP_ALLOWED_TARGET=

# The authorised team suffix. A mutating target MUST end with _<team> — this is
# the gate that stops a deploy landing on a live team by mistake, and it holds
# even if the box allow-list above is ever widened. Both gates must pass. Fail
# closed: if this is unset, every mutating verb is refused. Example: t02
CTP_ALLOWED_TEAM=

# The MUTATING verbs the wrapper will run. Allow-list: a verb not named here is
# refused. Read verbs (host list <box>, project update-inventory) are reachable
# separately and always confirmed; host vars, redeploy and remove are not
# reachable, and secrets/make are refused outright regardless of this line.
CTP_ALLOWED_VERBS=deploy deploy-role

# Container override. Leave blank to derive catapult-<current-user> at runtime.
CTP_CONTAINER=

# Optional: the project directory INSIDE the container that ctp runs from (where
# playbook.yml lives), e.g. /srv/inventories/<project>. Leave blank to translate
# the directory you invoke the wrapper from through the container's bind mount at
# runtime (run the wrapper from your range checkout). Set this only to pin a fixed
# project regardless of CWD.
CTP_PROJECT_DIR=

# Secret paths the hook refuses to let any tool read (Read tool or a Bash command
# naming the path), so their contents never reach the transcript. Space-separated
# glob patterns. Tilde expands to the target user's home. Extend per box; never
# remove the vault password or private-key entries.
CTP_SECRET_PATHS=/var/tmp/vlt_pf ~/.ssh/id_* ~/.zsh_history ~/.bash_history **/.env

# Org PII / intellectual-property paths the hook refuses to let any tool read OR
# write (Read/Write/Edit, or a Bash command naming the path), so Org data never
# reaches Claude's transcript. Same glob syntax as CTP_SECRET_PATHS (tilde
# expands to the target home; a **/ prefix matches anywhere by basename).
#
# Separate from CTP_SECRET_PATHS on purpose: secrets are credentials (never
# touch); these are a data-governance boundary you tune independently. Hooks
# cannot redact tool OUTPUT, so the only reliable protection for a file's
# contents is to deny the read here — list the directories where Org data lives.
#
# EMPTY IS THE DEFAULT (opt-in): nothing is denied until you add paths. Example:
#   CTP_PII_PATHS=~/org-data/** ~/cases/**/*.csv /srv/customer/**
CTP_PII_PATHS=
